<?nvs
// Shows how to check that a service account can use this configuration before you install the service.
// This program runs under the `nvs.toml` in its folder. That file includes `production.toml`.

// A server starts only if its account or an administrator owns each configuration file and its folder.
// No group of ordinary accounts may have write access to them.

// `nvs config check --as www-data nvs.toml` runs this check for the account `www-data`.
// If both files pass, it prints this line and exits with `0`:
//     ok: 2 files, 4 directives set, 1 override, 0 warnings

// If a file fails, it prints error `E0607` with the file name and exits with `1`.
// The help line of the error shows the command that repairs the file.

// On Windows, you can name the account of a service before the service exists:
//     nvs config check --as "NT SERVICE\shop" nvs.toml

// A server that passes the check reads the same values as this program. This prints `128M` and `30s`.
echo 'memory:     ', Core\Config::get('limits.memory') ?? '(unset)', "\n";
echo 'time limit: ', Core\Config::get('limits.wall_time') ?? '(unset)', "\n";
